---
/**
 * The masthead, on every tool page.
 *
 * An identity strip, not a navigation bar: the logo and the name of the thing,
 * and nothing else. Navigation lives on the page - the index *is* the list of
 * tools, and each tool page carries a single `.sm-back` to it. The link out to
 * seattlemakers.org lives in the footer, inside a sentence that gives it a
 * reason to be there.
 *
 * The logo is the one-line lockup - "Skinny", in the brand kit's naming - which
 * is the right shape for a 48px bar where the stacked one is not. Two things
 * about that artwork are worth knowing:
 *
 * - The kit's "dark" suffix means *for dark backgrounds*, not dark ink.
 *   `Logo final_Skinny dark.png` sets SEATTLE in pure white and is invisible on
 *   this white bar; `Logo final_Skinny.png` sets it in pure black. Established
 *   by sampling pixels - the two are indistinguishable in a file listing, and
 *   the white one looks like a blank canvas in a previewer.
 * - It is a lockup, so it already says "Seattle Makers". "Digital Toolbox"
 *   follows it as the product name, behind a divider, rather than the bar
 *   setting the organisation's name twice.
 *
 * This used to pick between the lockup and a monogram-plus-text fallback with
 * an `existsSync` check, because the artwork was not in the repo. It is now,
 * so there is one path.
 *
 * Styles live in global.css, not in a scoped block here, so the bar can be
 * reasoned about alongside the sheet it sits on.
 */
---

<header class="sm-masthead no-print">
  <div class="sm-masthead-inner">
    <a class="sm-brand" href="/">
      <img class="sm-lockup" src="/brand/wordmark-inline.png" alt="Seattle Makers" />
      <span class="sm-brand-name sm-brand-product">Digital Toolbox</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</header>
